---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Masthead from '../components/Masthead.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="open-bench — about"
  description="Why open-bench exists: a weekly head-to-head between coding LLMs, run on the same task with the same harness, scored by hidden tests and by the models themselves."
>
  <Header subtitle="about" />

  <Masthead
    title="about"
    subtitle="Pick the right coding model without trusting a vendor leaderboard."
  />

  <article class="prose about-prose">
    <p>
      <strong>open-bench</strong> is a weekly head-to-head between every major coding LLM I
      can get an API key for. Same blank repo, same <code>SPEC.md</code>, same hidden test
      suite. Each round produces a single ranked review &mdash; who passed, who didn't,
      who was cheapest, who was fastest, who other models thought wrote the cleanest code.
    </p>

    <p>
      I built it because I kept reaching for vendor leaderboards when picking a model
      to drop into an agent, and the numbers there never matched what I saw in
      practice. They benchmark one-shot prompts; I run multi-turn agents. They report
      pass@1 on synthetic problems; I care about wall-clock, dollars, and whether the
      output reads like something a teammate would write. open-bench is a small, opinionated
      answer to that &mdash; one task, one harness, one set of hidden tests, run against
      everyone at once. No marketing pages, no fine print. Just artifacts.
    </p>

    <p>
      How a round works: every model in the lineup gets the same task and an isolated
      git worktree, and writes its implementation through opencode. A hidden pytest
      battery runs against each output as the objective gate. Then every model in the
      lineup judges every other implementation (blinded), so the qualitative score
      comes from peer consensus rather than my taste. Cost, tokens, and wall-clock are
      tracked through the harness. Self-bias is measured but excluded from the
      headline median. The full output of a round &mdash; transcripts, diffs, judge
      rubrics, scores &mdash; is committed to the repo so anyone can replay or audit it.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the methodology in detail (task config, scoring, forking it for your own
      lineup, limitations) read <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/blob/main/ABOUT.md">ABOUT.md</a>.
      For the harness internals see <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/tree/main/bench/scripts">bench/scripts</a>,
      and the round artifacts live under <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/tree/main/builds">builds/</a>
      and <a href="https://github.com/anfocic/open-bench/tree/main/results">results/</a>.
      Latest results: <a href="/">home</a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-prose { max-width: 68ch; margin-top: 8px; }
  .about-prose p { margin: 14px 0; }
</style>
